CAT = "oacpPolicy"; SEG = "POL"
Q = [
("Service policy says a cruiser must be refuelled when the gauge reads below one quarter, unless the officer is on a priority call. Constable Ruiz clears a noise complaint with the gauge at one fifth and no call waiting. What should she do?", "Refuel before the next call", ["Wait until the gauge is empty", "Refuel at the end of the shift", "Report the gauge to dispatch"], "One fifth is below one quarter and she is not on a priority call, so the rule applies now. Waiting until the end of the shift ignores the stated trigger."),
("To join the bike unit, an officer needs two years of service, a current first aid certificate and no more than one missed qualification day this year. Which officer is eligible?", "Lee: 3 years, certified, 1 missed day", ["Park: 4 years, not certified, 0 missed", "Ng: 1 year, certified, 0 missed", "Hill: 5 years, certified, 2 missed"], "Lee meets all three conditions; one missed day is allowed. Hill has the service and certificate but two missed days is over the limit."),
("Dispatch ranks calls this way: first, any threat to life; second, a crime in progress; third, a crime already over; fourth, everything else. Which call goes first?", "A driver trapped in a car fire", ["A break-in happening right now", "A theft reported from last night", "A parking complaint downtown"], "A trapped driver in a fire is a threat to life, the top category. A break-in happening now is a crime in progress, which ranks second."),
("Three officers, Adams, Brar and Cole, each patrolled one of Bay Street, King Street and Elm Street. Brar was not on Bay Street. Cole was on King Street. Who was on Bay Street?", "Constable Adams", ["Constable Brar", "Constable Cole", "Cannot be determined"], "Cole is on King and Brar is not on Bay, so Brar must be on Elm, leaving Bay for Adams. The facts are enough, so it can be determined."),
("Every officer who works nights is given a parking pass. Constable Ito does not have a parking pass. What can be said of the statement \"Ito works nights\"?", "It is definitely false", ["It is definitely true", "It cannot be determined", "It is true only sometimes"], "If Ito worked nights, Ito would have a pass. Ito has none, so Ito does not work nights. It is not undetermined, because the rule covers every night worker."),
("Service policy says a detainee's property bag must be sealed and signed by the detainee, unless the detainee refuses, in which case a second officer signs as witness. Mr. Bell refuses to sign. What should the officer do?", "Get a second officer to sign", ["Leave the bag unsealed overnight", "Sign it on Mr. Bell's behalf", "Call the duty inspector in"], "The policy covers a refusal directly: a second officer witnesses. Calling the duty inspector is not what the policy asks for."),
("Service policy says an officer may take a marked cruiser home only if on call that night and living within 30 km of the station. Constable Moss is on call and lives 42 km away. May she take the cruiser home?", "No, it is not permitted", ["Yes, it is permitted", "Cannot be determined", "Only with written approval"], "Both conditions must be met and 42 km is over the 30 km limit. The policy mentions no approval that could override the distance rule."),
("Service policy requires a written report for any use of force that causes an injury or involves a weapon being drawn. Which situation needs a report?", "Baton drawn, no one hurt", ["Handcuffs applied, no injury", "Verbal warning to a crowd", "Escort by the arm, no injury"], "Drawing a baton is drawing a weapon, so a report is needed even with no injury. Handcuffing without injury meets neither condition."),
("All officers on the marine unit hold a boat licence. Constable Shaw holds a boat licence. What can be said of the statement \"Shaw is on the marine unit\"?", "It cannot be determined", ["It is definitely true", "It is definitely false", "It is true only sometimes"], "The rule says marine officers have licences, not that every licence holder is on the unit. Shaw may or may not be, so it is not definitely true."),
("Service policy says overtime of up to 4 hours in a week needs a sergeant's approval and anything over 4 hours needs an inspector's. Constable Diaz has 3 hours of overtime this week and is asked to stay 2 more. Whose approval is needed?", "An inspector's approval", ["A sergeant's approval", "No approval at all", "Both a sergeant and the union"], "Three plus two is 5 hours, which is over 4, so an inspector must approve. A sergeant's approval fits only if the 2 new hours are counted alone."),
("Constables Grant, Hoy and Iqbal started shifts at 06:00, 07:00 and 08:00, one each. Hoy started after Grant. Iqbal started before Grant. Who started at 07:00?", "Constable Grant", ["Constable Hoy", "Constable Iqbal", "Constable Hoy or Grant"], "Iqbal is before Grant and Grant is before Hoy, so the order is Iqbal at 06:00, Grant at 07:00 and Hoy at 08:00. Hoy is last, not middle."),
("Service policy says a ride-along guest must be 18 or older, unless the guest is a cadet in the service's own youth program. Which guest may ride along?", "A 16-year-old service cadet", ["A 17-year-old high school student", "A 16-year-old son of an officer", "A 15-year-old in a scout troop"], "The cadet is under 18 but fits the stated exception. Being an officer's son is not an exception, so that guest is still too young."),
("Service policy says officers may use the station gym during a shift only if their call queue is empty and a supervisor has been told. Constable Lau has no calls waiting and has told her sergeant. May she use the gym?", "Yes, it is permitted", ["No, it is not permitted", "Cannot be determined", "Only with written approval"], "Both conditions are met: an empty queue and a supervisor told. The policy does not ask for written approval, only that a supervisor be told."),
("A road closure policy says: close the lane first, then set out flares, then direct traffic, then take statements. An officer has just closed the lane. What comes next?", "Set out the flares", ["Take witness statements", "Start directing traffic", "Call for a tow truck"], "Flares come right after closing the lane. Directing traffic is the step after the flares, not before."),
("No officer may carry a personal phone in the cell block. Constable Ward is in the cell block and is following the rule. What can be said of the statement \"Ward is not carrying a personal phone\"?", "It is definitely true", ["It is definitely false", "It cannot be known", "It is true only sometimes"], "Anyone in the cell block who follows the rule has no personal phone, so the statement must be true. Nothing is left unknown."),
("Service policy says any officer who finds an unattended bag in the station lobby must keep people back and alert the duty sergeant at once. Constable Ortiz finds one. What should he do?", "Clear the lobby, alert the sergeant", ["Notify the sergeant at end of shift", "Ask the front desk clerk to check it", "Open the bag to see what is inside"], "The policy says to keep people back and alert the sergeant at once. Telling the sergeant later is the right person at the wrong time."),
("Service policy lets a constable release a found bicycle to anyone who shows proof of ownership, such as a receipt with the serial number. Ms. Tan brings a receipt whose serial number matches. What should the constable do?", "Release the bicycle to Ms. Tan", ["Hold it for a sergeant's review", "Keep it for 30 more days", "Ask her for a second receipt"], "A matching receipt is exactly the proof the policy names, so the constable can release it. The policy asks for no sergeant's review."),
("A constable may act as a coach officer with at least four years of service and the coach course finished, or with eight years of service. Which constable qualifies?", "Roy: 9 years, no course", ["Sim: 5 years, no course", "Tse: 3 years, course done", "Kaur: 7 years, no course"], "Roy has more than eight years, which qualifies without the course. Sim has over four years but has not finished the course."),
("At a scene, Officer Vance arrived before Officer Wu but after Officer Yates. Officer Zane arrived last. Who arrived second?", "Officer Vance", ["Officer Wu", "Officer Yates", "Officer Zane"], "The order is Yates, Vance, Wu, Zane. Wu came after Vance, so Wu was third."),
("Some officers in the traffic unit are certified radar operators. Constable Bloom is in the traffic unit. What can be said of the statement \"Bloom is a certified radar operator\"?", "It is not known", ["It must be true", "It must be false", "It is true only sometimes"], "Only some traffic officers are certified, so Bloom may or may not be one. It would be true only if all of them were certified."),
("Service policy says a found wallet may be returned without a report if it holds less than $50 and its owner is known. A wallet found by Constable Kim holds $30. May Kim return it without a report?", "Not enough information", ["Yes, it is permitted", "No, it is not permitted", "Only with written approval"], "The amount meets the rule, but nothing says whether the owner is known. Both conditions are needed, so a yes cannot be given yet."),
("All officers who passed the March firearms course are cleared for range duty. Constable Pratt is not cleared for range duty. What can be said of the statement \"Pratt passed the March course\"?", "It must be false", ["It must be true", "It cannot be known", "It is true only sometimes"], "Passing the course means being cleared. Pratt is not cleared, so Pratt did not pass. The rule leaves nothing unknown here."),
("Policy says higher priority calls go first, and two calls of the same priority go in the order received. Two priority-two calls came in at 21:14 and 21:09. A priority-one call came in at 21:20. Which call is handled second?", "The 21:09 priority-two call", ["The 21:14 priority-two call", "The 21:20 priority-one call", "Whichever call is nearest"], "The priority-one call goes first despite arriving last. Of the priority-two calls, 21:09 came in before 21:14, so it is second."),
("Every cruiser on the night shift carries a defibrillator. Car 14 is on the night shift. What can be said of the statement \"Car 14 carries a defibrillator\"?", "It must be true", ["It must be false", "It is not certain", "It is true at times"], "The rule covers every night shift cruiser, and Car 14 is one, so it carries a defibrillator. Nothing is left undetermined."),
("Service policy allows two officers to swap shifts if they hold the same rank and the swap is entered at least seven days ahead. Constables Ali and Bose enter a swap five days ahead. What should the sergeant do?", "Refuse the shift swap", ["Approve the shift swap", "Approve it as a favour", "Refer it to an inspector"], "The rank condition is met but five days is less than seven, so the swap fails the policy. Referring it to an inspector adds a step the policy does not call for."),
("Policy says an officer may give a member of the public a ride in a cruiser only to take them to a place of safety, such as a station or shelter, or to a hospital. Constable Grey finds a lost, cold child at night and drives her to the station to wait for her parents. Was the ride allowed?", "Yes, the ride was allowed", ["No, it was not allowed", "Not enough information", "Only if a parent agreed first"], "Taking a lost, cold child to the station is taking her to safety, which the policy permits. No parental agreement is required by the policy."),
("Service policy says a prisoner transport needs two officers, unless the prisoner is a youth under 16 travelling with a parent, when one officer may drive. Which transport may one officer do alone?", "A 15-year-old with his mother", ["A 17-year-old with her father", "A 15-year-old with no parent", "An adult with a family friend"], "The 15-year-old is under 16 and has a parent along, which fits the exception. The 17-year-old has a parent but is too old for it."),
("Policy says equipment problems go on the report in order of urgency: anything affecting officer safety, then anything affecting the radio, then anything cosmetic. Which goes on the report first?", "A torn body armour strap", ["A radio that drops calls", "A scratch on the rear door", "A faded unit number decal"], "Body armour is officer safety, the top category. A radio that drops calls is a radio problem, which comes second."),
]
